## Feature #5 Increment 3 (spec §4.5) — LEAK-FREE DROP-ON-CANCEL mid-stream: a
## source stream OWNS a C-side resource (here a `state_ptr`, standing in for a
## reactor registration / fd) and releases it in `def drop`. When the consumer
## stops pulling MID-STREAM and the stream goes out of scope, the source's
## `def drop` fires EXACTLY ONCE — leak-free teardown by ownership, no GC.
##
## `ResourceStream` is UNBOUNDED (it yields forever). `pull_some` drives it a
## FIXED number of times via the reactor-aware poll loop (a free-fn driver
## taking `&var` so the stream is not consumed/moved), then returns — leaving
## the stream PARTIALLY consumed (mid-iteration, `n == 2`, never exhausted). At
## `main` scope exit `src` drops, firing `ruxen_stream_res_free` → `res freed
## tag=7` prints once. Proves: a partially-drained stream releases its owned
## resource on drop, no leak, no double-free.
##
## v1 DRIVER NOTE: the drive is a free fn taking `&var ResourceStream`, NOT the
## mixin's `for_each` method. Calling a `&var self` mixin method (`src.for_each`)
## directly on a local taints it OUT of drop-elaboration (the conservative-
## dealloc limit Scope/Queue also hit), suppressing the scope-exit drop; passing
## the stream by explicit `&var` borrow to a driver does NOT taint it. So the
## leak-free-on-cancel guarantee holds for the borrow-driver path; the
## `for_each`-then-drop path is the drop-rooting follow-up (a v2 item, same as
## the other concurrency features).

class ResourceStream
  state_ptr: Int
  n: Int
  include Stream
  type Item = Int

  def init(tag: Int)
    self.state_ptr = ruxen_stream_res_new(tag)
    self.n = 0
  end

  def var poll_next(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[Option[Int]]
    self.n = self.n + 1
    Poll.Ready(Some(self.n))
  end

  def drop
    if self.state_ptr != 0
      ruxen_stream_res_free(self.state_ptr)
      self.state_ptr = 0
    end
  end
end

## Pull `count` elements off the stream via the reactor-aware poll loop, printing
## each. Takes the stream by `&var` borrow (does NOT consume it), so the caller's
## `src` still drops at scope exit.
def pull_some(s: &var ResourceStream, count: Int) -> nil
  var cx = Context.executor
  var pulled = 0
  while pulled < count
    match (&var s).poll_next(&var cx)
      Poll.Ready(item) ->
        match item
          Some(v) -> puts "got #{v}"
          nil     -> pulled = count
        end
      Poll.Pending -> Scope.pump_or_park
    end
    pulled = pulled + 1
  end
  nil
end

def main
  puts "stream start"
  var src = ResourceStream.new(7)
  pull_some(&var src, 2)
  puts "cancelled mid-stream"
  puts "stream done"
end

## A tiny C resource so the source's `def drop` has an observable, free-tracked
## side effect (`res freed tag=N`).
lib "runtime/stream.c"
  def ruxen_stream_res_new(tag: Int) -> Int
  def ruxen_stream_res_free(state_ptr: Int) -> nil
end
